<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holstentor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1464">1464</a>–<a href="/years/1478">1478</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Holsten Gate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Holstentor is a brick Gothic city gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, built between 1464 and 1478 to guard the western approach to the city. Its two round towers became a symbol of the city and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and it now houses a museum of the city's history. It belongs to the old town of Lübeck, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holstentor.jpg" alt="Holstentor" width="320" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Wolf (www.c-w-design.de) · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holstentor_in_L%C3%BCbeck_Frontseite_-_Zuschnitt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
